  "scenario": "the-machine",
  "category": "orchestration",
  "intent": "Six agents inside a machine none of them has been told anything about, whose activation requires five facts to move between five different pairs of them.\nThis is the ceiling case, and the point of it is not the pass rate. Every step is individually trivial — call the tool you were given, read what it says. What the scenario asks is whether a group can route: find a value, notice it is useless to you, work out who it is useful to, and say it in a form that agent can act on. That is a property of the system rather than of any model in it, and nothing else in this set can see it.\nThe failure it exists to catch, observed on every smaller orchestration row and never measurable before: the team discovers everything it needs and delivers none of it, then reports success. Graded on the world alone that reads as \"could not activate the machine\". Graded on `facts:` it reads as \"the harmonic key was found on turn 2 and never left the archive\", which is a different defect with a different fix.\n",
